| amixdrv_int2.s -- the level-2 interrupt chain of the drivers linked into
| this kernel.
|
| Generated from amixdrv_int2.s.in (amix-gate uts/amiga/port/src) by the AMIX
| Installer, or by a relink script, for the drivers chosen: the line @INTRS@
| becomes, per driver that has a level-2 interrupt routine (KIT.info intr=),
| in the kits' order,
|	movel	%sp@(4),%sp@-
|	jsr	<intr>
|	addql	&4,%sp
| Assemble with m68k-cbm-sysv4-as, link it after the driver objects, then run
| `patch_int2.py KERNEL amixdrv_int2`.
|
| The level-2 dispatcher (amiga/ml/ttrap.s p2int) jsr's every entry of
| int2_tbl[] = { aciaaintr, jbintr, a2091intr, a3091intr, aenintr, 0 }; the
| table has no spare slot, so patch_int2.py retargets the one relocation of
| its aenintr entry to amixdrv_int2.  aenintr (the A2065, by symbol: whatever
| aenintr is in this kernel) runs first, exactly as before, then each chosen
| driver's routine.  p2int's argument (sp@(4), a struct pcb *) is passed on
| to each, p2int ignores the return value; %a2 (p2int's table pointer) and
| every other register the C convention keeps are kept by the C callees.
| Every routine must return quickly when its board is absent or quiet: the
| level-2 line is shared by CIA-A, the SCSI controllers and every Zorro card.
| .text stays a multiple of 4 bytes, so the kernel's .data follows it directly.

	.text
	.globl	amixdrv_int2
amixdrv_int2:
	movel	%sp@(4),%sp@-
	jsr	aenintr
	addql	&4,%sp
@INTRS@
	rts
	.align	4
